WEBVTT

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:26.090
Your tone has become a lot
more refined, Homura-san.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:31.060
Going to flute classes made practising
long tones and scales more fun.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.160
Atta girl.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.720
I'm doing my best!

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.000
You've got guts, if nothing else.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.760
"Nothing else"?! You know that's not true!

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.220
Surround her! Don't let her escape!

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.900
How dare you get violent with an
innocent girl? Have you no shame?!

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.170
I'll take you to court!

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.800
That's rich, coming from an illegal trespasser!

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:00.530
What's the big deal?

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.080
I'm gonna join this school next month, anyway!

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.290
Hey, concert band! Help me out here!

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:07.370
Concert band?

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.020
Don't worry.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.890
This guy is Hinohara-san,
our student council president.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.420
Is that a Sakuragaoka Junior High uniform?

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:16.510
Yeah...

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.920
That's a trombone, right?

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:20.680
Judging from its size, a bass trombone?

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.160
That's right!

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.850
Could you be Kamijou Haruta-san?

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:26.410
Yeah, why?

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.270
I'm Gotou Akari!

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.310
There's a mystery I want you to solve!

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.360
That's a cool invention.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.530
You get it?

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:11.250
Of course!

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:13.710
Hey...

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.170
They still need a lot of work.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:18.050
Inventions like those would make
Alexander Graham Bell cry!

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.520
S-Sir!

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.330
Please forgive us!

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:21.900
We'll get right to it!

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.480
That's enough. You're being an eyesore.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:24.320
Let's go, Suguru.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.120
We should head back and resume the
Snake Robot Mk2's launch experiments.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.690
Don't make anything stupid!

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:30.820
Who were that miserable duo?

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.610
The Hagimoto brothers,
from the inventor club.

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.190
Two of the top ten students
on the student council blacklist.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.210
The top ten on the blacklist?

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.640
If I said Nagoe from the drama club
was among that number,

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.620
would that help you get
what kind of list it is?

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:43.820
Oh...

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.580
So, how'd you know about me?

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.740
You're famous among the
members of our concert band.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.540
Because he was living at school
as the biology club's pet?

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.050
Living at school?

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.960
Of course not! She's kidding!

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.090
Quit running your mouth, Chika-chan.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:04.400
Gotou-san, what exactly
are they saying about Haruta?

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.120
That he's a detective of sorts
who solves all kinds of mysteries.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:07.930
Wow.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.270
Are you going to join the
concert band in high school too?

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:12.850
That's the plan.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:15.280
Hooray! You're part of our family already.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.190
Shall I play a bit?

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.320
Such a pretty tone.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:28.940
It's not easy to tongue
the bass trombone correctly.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:30.200
I guess you have a talent for it.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:32.260
No, no.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.270
But last year,

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:37.130
I made our band pick Benny Goodman's Medley
as our free-choice piece with my skills!

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:40.710
Doesn't it have a trombone solo?

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.380
It was no big deal.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:46.920
I left the audience at the
competition venue speechless.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.100
I think I have a decent grasp of
your skills and personality now.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.660
You figured it out?

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.520
That's Detective Kamijou-senpai for you!

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.610
Well, I'm sure Detective Haruta
will be happy to help you out.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.950
It's your job to solve absurd problems like
the ones Princess Kaguya gave her suitors.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:03.940
So what's this mystery?

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.080
Um...

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.910
What colour is Elephant's Breath?

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.500
This dictionary has more than
seven thousand colour samples.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.930
But it only has the name
"Elephant's Breath". No sample.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:24.510
I didn't know there was a colour
with such a funny name.

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:29.470
Apparently the term was first
introduced around 1884.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.430
Looking at your own breath
isn't going to help.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.890
Why do you want to know about it?

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:42.820
That jerk... He said he saw Elephant's Breath.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:47.150
All my life, I'd been told that
my grandfather was dead.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.320
But we found out that he was actually alive.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:53.600
I don't want to admit he's my grandfather,
so let me refer to him as "that jerk".

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:58.380
He and my grandmother promised
to marry a long time ago.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.370
But that jerk wanted to study art,

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.360
so he used the money they'd
both saved up and went to America.

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:09.280
My grandmother later found out
that she was pregnant with his child,

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.390
but she didn't want to worry that jerk,
so she never informed him.

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:16.650
She waited for him, believing that
he'd return after a year as promised.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:19.440
But she never heard from him after that.

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:22.360
It was only when a friend
got in touch last year

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:24.800
that we found that jerk was here.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:26.780
Grandma!

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.880
Meet the high schoolers
I told you about yesterday.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.240
Thank you for taking care of my granddaughter.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:33.750
Come in.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:36.700
Wow.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.960
Did your grandfather paint all these?

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:40.810
That's right.

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:43.860
Oh, my granddaughter.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.500
Good of you to come.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:47.390
This is my grandfather. Technically.

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:48.480
Hello.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:49.760
Um, we are—

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:53.350
Oh? High school girls these days
sure have long legs.

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.010
Stop staring, you perverted geezer!

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:57.610
Apparently he's got amnesia.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:01.310
He has next to no memory
of his time in America.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.810
The only thing he does remember

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.260
is that he saw Elephant's Breath.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:07.990
Elephant's Breath?

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:09.640
Knock it off!

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:11.900
Try asking him about his time there.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.660
He might remember something.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:15.950
Got it.

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:22.050
There isn't anything for me to remember.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.700
Looks like he really
can't remember anything.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:28.070
Do you know when he left for the States?

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:29.720
Uh...

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:34.130
He left for San Francisco in the year Showa 41.

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:36.680
1966, huh?

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:38.640
Such a beautiful painting.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:39.890
This is pointillism, right?

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:41.850
Seurat.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:45.480
Grande Jatte.

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:48.400
Surer... Grand Chat?

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.720
Seurat was a painter, Chika-chan.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:53.280
He's responsible for many
unique works of pointillism.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.660
One of his paintings, "A Sunday Afternoon
on the Island of La Grande Jatte",

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:58.330
is on display at the Art Institute of Chicago.

00:07:58.330 --> 00:07:59.970
Am I the only ignorant one here?

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:02.120
Well, I lived in Chicago for a while.

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:04.850
You went from San Francisco to Chicago?

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.940
I'm starting to think I did,

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:10.090
my granddaughter.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:12.620
Take this seriously!

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:14.050
There, there.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.350
Those are your paintings too, aren't they?

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:20.470
That's right.

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:25.940
Do you mind if I take a look?

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:29.360
They're not worth looking at.

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:31.770
What's the big deal?

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:35.150
Only the colour of the sky is different, huh?

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:37.320
Yellow, orange, black...

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.950
One does look like dawn,

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:42.540
but the other two seem to be dusk and night.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:46.410
You returned to Japan in 1976, right?

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:48.940
Hmm... I don't know.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:49.590
That's right.

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:53.470
So these were painted
immediately after his return?

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:57.640
The elephant's breath
doesn't seem to be painted.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.640
I'm a little tired.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:03.900
I guess a young girl's legs were
too dazzling for my weary eyes.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:07.190
There you go dodging
the topic again, you fraud!

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:09.110
Sorry, but could you let him rest?

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:11.570
Of course. We'll be leaving now.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:13.110
Excuse us.

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:16.450
I can't forgive him.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:19.440
He left my grandma alone for all those years,

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:22.120
and yet he hasn't even apologised.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:24.830
He didn't seem to be a bad guy, though.

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:27.080
He's a selfish fraud!

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:30.190
Despite hurting my grandma so much,

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:32.750
he showed up the moment he fell ill...

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:36.930
He's taking advantage of her kindness
to have her take care of him.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.430
Have a bite. Calm down.

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:45.950
I don't know if he really doesn't remember,
or if he's just faking it,

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:50.860
but I'm definitely going to make him regret
his actions and apologise for them!

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:54.440
Didn't he say anything
about Elephant's Breath?

00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:00.570
I repeatedly grilled him
on what he did in America.

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:03.450
I saw Elephant's Breath.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:05.700
What's that supposed to mean?

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:08.580
Kipling.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.280
Kipling...

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:12.000
Rudyard Kipling, maybe?

00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:13.690
A painter?

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:15.770
A novelist, Chika-chan.

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:23.350
In a land far, far away, there was an elephant
that escaped from human captivity.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:28.600
Because he reeked of humans, and had a
broken chain dangling noisily from his back leg,

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:34.980
every herd he met was too
wary of him to let him join.

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:39.570
In despair, the elephant came to
hate humans and attacked their villages,

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:40.950
trampling them beneath his feet.

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:46.200
But one day, he met a baby elephant
that had been separated from its herd.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:51.220
He raised the innocent and
trusting child as his own.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:55.320
Eventually, the baby elephant
grew up and asked him,

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:59.350
"Father, what is this chain?"

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:02.180
The elephant answered,

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:06.700
"This is my sorrow...
The curse I have been burdened with."

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:13.650
And then, the grown-up baby elephant used
his trunk to destroy the old elephant's chain.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.770
How does this have anything
to do with Elephant's Breath?

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:18.600
Any ideas, Haruta?

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:19.590
Nope.

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:22.490
All we know now is that in 1966,

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:25.680
he went to San Francisco in the United States,

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:28.920
and later saw Seurat's painting in Chicago, maybe?

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:34.370
And in 1976, he returned to Japan
and painted those three paintings.

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:37.050
What were his paintings like?

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:39.050
Here you go.

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:42.400
Where could he have seen this elephant?

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:45.760
Chicago has the Lincoln Park Zoo.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:49.200
The only difference between these
three paintings is the colour of the sky.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:50.680
That's been bothering me too.

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:53.600
Yellow, orange, black...

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:58.740
Don't pry any further into these paintings.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:01.190
Am I clear?

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:17.080
Kusakabe-sensei had an unusually
scary look on his face back there.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:19.400
What could those paintings mean?

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.110
I want to know...

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:22.150
What do we do?

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:25.260
We can't do anything.
Not after Sensei told us to back off.

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:27.760
I'll look into it some more.

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:32.090
If he's bound by any chains,
we should take them off for him.

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:35.340
You've got a point there.

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:36.390
Yeah.

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:41.200
Then how about we invite Gotou-san
and go see an elephant's breath?

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:50.310
An elephant's breath isn't very visible...

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:51.120
Yeah.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:56.720
Did you ask your grandfather
about the paintings?

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:58.360
I grilled him nice and good.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:01.650
But he dodged the question again.

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:06.630
That jerk's trying to take his silence to the grave.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:10.620
Is he that sick?

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:14.560
Apparently he's got a bunch of illnesses
and doesn't have long left.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:18.340
So I have to hurry up and make him
remember what he did to my grandma,

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:21.400
give him a good lecture,
and make him kowtow to her in apology.

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:25.210
Okay. Time to eat, don't you think?

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:26.870
I made lunch.

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:27.990
Okay!

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:32.010
Ta-dah!

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:33.460
Whoa!

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:35.640
You can cook, huh, Homura-san?

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:38.420
I can only make fried egg!

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:40.830
Call me a cute chef, if you please.

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.450
Chika-chan's a good cook, if nothing else.

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:44.260
Give it back!

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:48.190
You don't deserve to eat my food!

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:50.410
Here you go, Maren.

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:51.010
Thanks.

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:52.390
You two as well?

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:56.830
Could this be a double date?

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:59.180
Th-That aside, did you see an elephant's breath?

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:02.220
Not at all.

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:05.450
Your grandfather went to the States in 1966, right?

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:10.780
Back then, there was a big rebellion opposing
the discrimination against African-Americans.

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:12.160
The Watts rebellion?

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.300
What started as riots in Los Angeles

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:18.890
spread to San Francisco, and later even Chicago,

00:14:18.890 --> 00:14:20.600
claiming many lives.

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:23.120
San Francisco to Chicago?

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:25.570
Isn't that the route your grandfather took?

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:30.980
Even Japanese-Americans and international
students got dragged into those riots.

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:32.480
So that jerk got dragged into it too?

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:37.010
Maybe he lost his job and
all his money due to the riots.

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:39.850
It's also possible he slept in a zoo.

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.620
Lincoln Park has no entry fee.

00:14:43.510 --> 00:14:47.040
A lot of people used to sneak in and sleep there.

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:51.990
So he experienced a morning at the zoo
and painted the elephant he saw then?

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:54.450
What about the changes in the sky's colour?

00:14:54.450 --> 00:15:00.500
Maybe it's a metaphor for his mental state
in America changing from hope to despair.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:04.290
But would Kusakabe-sensei have stopped us
so vehemently if that were the case?

00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:07.670
This doesn't look a zoo, though.

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:10.170
If I had to say, it's more like a jungle?

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:11.440
A safari park?

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:12.320
No way.
A safari park?

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:12.510
I don't think Chicago has one.
No way.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:14.300
I don't think Chicago has one.

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:15.970
I understand.

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:18.520
I'll go ask him now.

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:26.550
Were you unable to come back
because you got dragged into the riots?

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:30.900
You lived in a zoo in Chicago like some hobo,

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:34.320
and these paintings are of an elephant
you saw there one morning, right?

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:39.960
Tell me about school instead, my granddaughter.

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:44.110
People are seriously trying to help you, you know!

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:47.680
Apologise to Grandma and my seniors!

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.870
Stop it, Akari.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:09.390
How long are you going to keep this up?

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:15.530
Liars and frauds get the chance to own up
for what they did in life at the very end.

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:18.240
But you're different.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:20.290
Stop right there.

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:23.490
Somebody has to free you
from the chains shackling you.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:26.250
Please leave.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:28.610
You haven't been able
to sleep in peace, have you?

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:31.110
Even after you returned to Japan.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:34.370
That's right.

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:39.050
Post-traumatic stress disorder,
or PTSD for short.

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:43.310
People who are exposed
to severe psychological shock

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:46.230
sometimes become incapable
of processing their memories.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:50.590
And you've been tormented by
nightmares and flashbacks for forty years.

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:54.680
Does that mean you got
sick because of the riots,

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:56.650
and that's why you couldn't come back?

00:16:58.310 --> 00:16:59.400
Yeah...

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:08.410
That's it.

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:11.580
He got caught up in the riots
as soon as he got to America,

00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:14.420
lost the money he needed to study,

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:18.970
and when he tried to get back on his feet in Chicago,
he lost everything in the riots there.

00:17:19.910 --> 00:17:21.430
"Elephant's Breath..."

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:25.680
I wasn't referring to the colour,
but to the breath of a sleeping elephant.

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:33.730
Elephants are wary creatures, so they can
only sleep in places where they feel safe.

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:38.190
You saw yourself in the lonely elephant at the zoo?

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:43.160
A peaceful resting place
isn't something you find yourself,

00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:44.950
but something that's offered to you by others.

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:48.420
I realised that far too late.

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:03.720
All right. Let's stop here for today.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:06.030
Thank you very much!

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:09.690
Be sure to collect your
spring break practice schedules.

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:12.210
Spring break, huh?

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:13.680
I'm gonna be a third-year.

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:15.040
You'll have entrance exams soon.

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:18.290
Don't say it! Let us remain
second-years for a bit longer!

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:21.150
Not leaving, Haruta?

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:23.440
Haruta?

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:25.870
What's wrong? You're acting kinda weird.

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:29.660
You all go ahead.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:36.230
Sensei.

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:39.920
About those paintings the other day...

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:43.220
You looked it up, didn't you?

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:45.440
I'm sorry.

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:47.890
Didn't we already solve the case?

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:50.320
Haruta?

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:52.940
Knowing what those three paintings mean

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:56.110
might be too heavy a burden to carry alone.

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:58.090
Tell us, Haruta.

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:03.560
Normally, zoo elephants
sleep inside their shelters.

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:06.560
Visitors don't get to see them sleeping.

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:07.660
What do you mean?

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:07.670
He was able to see an elephant
sleep from close by...
What do you mean?

00:19:07.670 --> 00:19:12.220
He was able to see an elephant
sleep from close by...

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:14.150
in a jungle like the one in those paintings.

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:19.410
He was looking for a safe place
in the middle of hell on Earth.

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:21.000
He wasn't in America?

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:24.250
He was drafted into the Vietnam War.

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:26.560
No way!

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:32.190
I could see foreigners with permanent
residency getting conscripted, but...

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:39.910
Apparently a lot of young Japanese citizens
studying in America went to Vietnam.

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:43.440
It didn't matter if they had
a resident or tourist visa.

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:49.100
I intended to ask him directly
that day, but I couldn't.

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:52.950
The grandmother probably knows already.

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:57.920
Those weren't the painted
colours of dawn, dusk, and night.

00:19:58.290 --> 00:19:59.350
What's that?

00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:06.120
Agent Blue, Agent Orange, and Agent White.

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:07.430
While it was called blue,

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:09.670
the solution was actually yellow in colour.

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:11.110
Agent Orange was orange,

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:14.680
and Agent White referred to a black solution.

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:17.180
Yellow, orange, black...

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:18.850
They match the colours of the sky.

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:20.340
Not the sky.

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:22.320
They're the colours of the defoliants.

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:29.930
Defoliants are sprayed early in the morning,
when the temps are low and the winds calm.

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:35.910
The morning sky would've been dyed
either yellow, orange, or black then.

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:38.380
In Vietnam, Gotou-san's grandfather

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:42.780
might've had the chance to approach
an elephant used for manual labour.

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:46.550
Maybe the defoliant was sprayed then, and—

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:49.970
Gotou-san?!

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:51.010
How much of that did you hear?

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:53.220
Pretty much all of it.

00:20:54.490 --> 00:20:58.370
So he went to war...

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:01.450
and killed people?

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:04.790
That's not... I don't know.

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:09.200
Wars are brutal, merciless,
and foolish endeavours.

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:13.110
Even so, your grandfather
returned from one alive.

00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:18.410
Is that why my grandfather can't sleep at night?

00:21:18.410 --> 00:21:22.670
Is that why he doesn't say anything,
even when I badmouth him?

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:27.720
You still have time.

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:32.730
Isn't it your job to destroy
the elephant's cursed chain?

00:21:34.430 --> 00:21:40.940
The baby elephant grew up and asked him,
"Father, what is this chain?"

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:45.150
"This is my sorrow...
The curse I have been burdened with."

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:50.910
And then, the grown-up baby elephant used
his trunk to destroy the old elephant's chain.

00:21:53.860 --> 00:21:56.280
Why don't you play the trombone
for your grandfather?

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:01.310
Okay!

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:05.140
Gotou-san, you forgot this!

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:09.060
I'll come show it to you some other time!

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:12.090
Will she be okay?

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:16.080
I don't think there's any chain
that could stand up to Gotou-san.

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:16.650
Yeah.

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:33.450
Boys! We want boys!

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:35.210
I like how shameless you are.

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:36.680
I don't want your stupid sympathy.

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:39.870
That student is a spring illusion.
